P('R6',ty='match',title='Should children be given homework?',opts=[
 ['A','Educational psychologist','The research on homework is more nuanced than either side admits. For older students, a moderate amount is linked to better results, but for younger children the benefits are close to zero. What matters far more than quantity is purpose: tasks that simply repeat what was done in class achieve little. Homework should prepare students to learn something new, not just keep them busy. Parents, meanwhile, should be supporters, not supervisors.'],
 ['B','Secondary school teacher','I set homework because the school day is simply too short to cover the curriculum. Without independent practice, students forget most of what they learn. I accept that some tasks are poorly designed, and I have changed my own approach over the years. However, those who call for homework to be abolished entirely underestimate how much learning depends on repetition. Practice may be dull, but it works.'],
 ['C','Parent of three','Every evening in our house is a battle. My children come home exhausted, and then spend two hours on worksheets that seem to teach them nothing new. I end up doing half of it myself. I am not against learning at home, but it should be reading together or a project the children actually care about. The evidence that endless worksheets help young children is, as far as I can tell, non-existent.'],
 ['D','Head of a primary school','Five years ago we abolished homework for under-elevens, replacing it with a simple expectation that children read for pleasure each evening. Parents are asked only to show an interest, not to check or correct. I do not claim that homework itself was harmful – we simply wanted to try something different – and the results have been encouraging: reading levels have risen, and families report less stress. Older students preparing for exams may well need more structured independent work.']],
 q=[{'q':'Which writer agrees with writer C that research shows little benefit from homework for young children?','a':'A','tg':['inference'],'e':'A: küçük çocuklar için fayda "close to zero". C de kanıt olmadığını söylüyor. D ödevin zararlı olduğunu iddia etmiyor.'},
    {'q':'Which writer has a different view from writers A and C on the value of repetitive practice?','a':'B','tg':['attitude'],'e':'B tekrarın işe yaradığını savunuyor; A ve C tekrar eden ödevlerin az işe yaradığını söylüyor.'},
    {'q':'Which writer has a similar view to writer A about the role parents should play?','a':'D','tg':['inference'],'e':'A: veliler denetçi değil destekçi olmalı. D: velilerden yalnızca ilgi göstermeleri isteniyor, kontrol etmeleri değil. C ise ödevin yarısını kendisi yapıyor.'},
    {'q':'Which writer expresses a similar view to writer C about what kind of activity is useful at home?','a':'D','tg':['inference'],'e':'C: birlikte okuma; D: ödev yerine her akşam keyif için okuma.'}]),
P('R7',ty='gap',title='Rewilding: letting nature take over',p='When the Knepp estate in southern England stopped farming in 2001, its owners were facing financial ruin. The heavy clay soil had never been ideal for crops, and years of intensive agriculture had left the land exhausted.\n(1) ______\nThe idea was simple, if radical: rather than managing the land, they would let nature manage itself. Fences were removed, and small herds of cattle, pigs and deer were introduced to roam freely, imitating the large wild animals that once lived in Britain.\n(2) ______\nWithin a few years, the fields had begun to transform. Thorny bushes spread across former farmland, providing shelter for young trees. Ponds formed where pigs had dug up the ground.\n(3) ______\nPerhaps the most remarkable change was the return of species that had become rare across the country. Turtle doves, nightingales and purple emperor butterflies all appeared in significant numbers.\n(4) ______\nNot everyone was pleased, however. Neighbouring farmers complained that the estate looked untidy and worried that weeds would spread onto their land.\n(5) ______\nToday, Knepp is held up as a model of what is possible. It attracts thousands of visitors a year, and its income from tourism and nature-friendly meat now exceeds what it once earned from farming.\n(6) ______\nSupporters argue that if similar projects were adopted across the country, they could help reverse the decline in wildlife that has been one of the defining features of the last century.',
 opts=[['A','These animals turned out to be essential. By grazing, digging and trampling, they created a constantly changing mix of habitats, something no human manager could have planned.'],['B','Faced with this situation, the owners took a decision that many of their neighbours considered foolish.'],['C','For some, the sight of this "messy" landscape was a betrayal of centuries of careful land management.'],['D','Critics point out, though, that few landowners could afford to take such a risk, and that Britain still needs to produce its own food.'],['E','The estate is now home to one of the largest populations of nightingales in the country, at a time when the species has declined by over 90 per cent elsewhere.'],['F','Many of the animals were later sold to farms in other parts of Europe.'],['G','This mixture of open ground, scrub and water proved to be exactly what many insects and birds needed.']],
 a=['B','A','G','E','C','D'],tg=[['coherence'],['reference'],['reference'],['coherence'],['coherence'],['advanced-linkers']],
 e=['Mali çöküş ve yorgun toprak → "Faced with this situation ... decision": B; sonraki paragraf "The idea was simple".','Hayvanlar tanıtıldı → "These animals turned out to be essential": A.','Çalılar, ağaçlar, göletler → "This mixture of open ground, scrub and water": G.','Nadir türler geri döndü → bülbül nüfusu: E.','Çiftçiler şikâyet etti → bazıları için "messy" manzara ihanet: C.','Bugün model örnek → "Critics point out, though": D; ardından "Supporters argue". F fazladır.']),
