P('L2',ty='open',id='l2',title='Cümleler',sc=[('F','Hello everyone. I\'m a marine biologist, and for the last six years I\'ve been studying octopuses off the coast of Portugal. People often ask why I chose octopuses rather than dolphins or whales. Partly it was practical – octopuses live in shallow water, so they\'re easier to study. But mainly it\'s because they\'re so unlike us, and yet clearly intelligent. An octopus has around five hundred million neurons, and two thirds of them are in its arms rather than its head. This means each arm can, in a sense, make its own decisions. One of the things that surprised me most was how individual they are. Some are bold and curious, others extremely shy. I gave each of the octopuses I studied a name, which some colleagues find unscientific. One of them, whom I called Lola, learned to recognise me and would approach my diving mask. Octopuses are famous for escaping from aquariums. In one case, an octopus repeatedly turned off a light in its tank by squirting water at it. Sadly, octopuses have very short lives – most species live only one to two years. That\'s one of the great mysteries: why would evolution produce such a clever animal that lives so briefly? My current research focuses on how octopuses sleep. We\'ve found evidence that they may even dream, as their skin changes colour during certain stages of sleep.')],
 p='The speaker chose octopuses partly because they live in (1) ______ water.\nAbout two thirds of an octopus\'s neurons are located in its (2) ______.\nShe was surprised by the different (3) ______ of individual octopuses.\nSome colleagues think giving octopuses names is (4) ______.\nOne octopus learned to recognise the speaker\'s (5) ______.\nAn octopus in an aquarium turned off a (6) ______ using water.\nMost octopuses live for between one and (7) ______ years.\nDuring sleep, an octopus\'s (8) ______ may change colour.',
 a=[['shallow'],['arms'],['personalities','characters','personality'],['unscientific'],['diving mask','mask'],['light'],['two','2'],['skin']],
 tg=[['detail'],['detail'],['inference'],['detail'],['detail'],['detail'],['spelling'],['detail']],
 e=['"they live in shallow water".','"two thirds of them are in its arms".','"how individual they are. Some are bold ..., others extremely shy" → kişilik.','"which some colleagues find unscientific".','"would approach my diving mask".','"turned off a light in its tank".','"one to two years".','"their skin changes colour".']),
P('L3',ty='mc',id='l3',sc=[('M','My guest today is Dr Helen Park, an architect who specialises in designing schools. Helen, why schools?'),('F','I started out designing offices, and I found it rather unfulfilling. Then I was asked to work on a school extension, and I was struck by how little thought had gone into the existing building. Children were spending seven hours a day in rooms with poor light and terrible acoustics. I realised design could make a real difference to how they learn.'),('M','What do architects most often get wrong?'),('F','We tend to design for how a building looks rather than how it will be used. A glass atrium may win awards, but if it\'s too hot in summer and too noisy all year, it\'s a failure. I now spend weeks in schools before I draw anything, watching how students and teachers actually move around.'),('M','Is there evidence that design affects learning?'),('F','There is, though people sometimes overstate it. A good building won\'t turn a poor school into a great one. But studies do show that natural light, fresh air and the ability to control temperature have measurable effects on concentration. Those are relatively cheap to get right, which makes it all the more frustrating when they\'re ignored.'),('M','How involved are students in your designs?'),('F','Very. I used to consult them as a formality, if I\'m honest. But a group of eleven-year-olds once pointed out that the toilets I\'d planned were too far from the playground. It seemed trivial, but it would have caused chaos at break times. Since then, I take their views far more seriously.'),('M','What\'s the biggest challenge facing school design now?'),('F','Budgets, without doubt. Governments want buildings that are cheap to construct, but cheap buildings are often expensive to run. A bit more spent on insulation at the start saves enormous amounts on heating later. Persuading people to think long-term is the hardest part of my job.')],
 q=[q('Why did Helen start designing schools?',['She was bored with her previous work.','She had always been more interested in education than offices.','She realised existing schools were poorly designed.','She was offered a large contract.'],2,['detail'],'Mevcut binaya ne kadar az özen gösterildiğini fark etti; tasarımın fark yaratabileceğini anladı.'),
    q('What does Helen say architects often get wrong?',['They ignore the cost of buildings.','They focus on appearance rather than use.','They rely too much on glass.','They do not spend enough time consulting teachers.'],1,['detail'],'"design for how a building looks rather than how it will be used".'),
    q('What is Helen\'s view of the research on design and learning?',['It shows that design is the main factor in school success.','Its findings are sometimes exaggerated.','It has not produced any clear results.','It focuses too much on expensive features.'],1,['attitude'],'"though people sometimes overstate it".'),
    q('Why does Helen feel frustrated?',['Simple improvements are often not made.','Schools cannot afford fresh air systems.','Teachers do not control the temperature.','Studies are ignored by governments.'],0,['inference'],'Nispeten ucuz iyileştirmeler bile göz ardı ediliyor.'),
    q('What does the story about the toilets illustrate?',['Children often complain about small things.','Helen had made a serious error in a design.','Students\' opinions can be genuinely useful.','Break times are difficult to manage.'],2,['purpose'],'Bu olaydan sonra öğrencilerin görüşlerini çok daha ciddiye alıyor.'),
    q('What does Helen find most difficult about her job?',['working with small budgets','convincing people to consider future costs','designing efficient heating systems','meeting strict government regulations on safety'],1,['detail'],'"Persuading people to think long-term is the hardest part".')]),
