WX=[{'id':'c1x-w1','part':1,'kind':'essay','t':'Part 1 · Deneme: Şehir merkezlerinde araçlar',
  'in':'Your class has attended a panel discussion on how to make city centres better places to live. You have made the notes below. Write an essay discussing TWO of the methods in your notes. You should explain which method you think is more effective, giving reasons in support of your answer. You may, if you wish, make use of the opinions expressed in the discussion, but you should use your own words as far as possible. Write 220–260 words.',
  'mail':'Methods for improving city centres:\n• banning private cars\n• creating more green spaces\n• encouraging people to live in the centre\n\nSome opinions expressed in the discussion:\n"Without cars, shops in the centre will lose customers."\n"Parks make people want to spend time in a city."\n"City centres are empty and unsafe at night because nobody lives there."',
  'pts':WP(('İlk yöntemi tartış',['car','traffic','vehicle','ban','pedestrian','green space','park','live in the centre','residents']),('İkinci yöntemi tartış',['whereas','on the other hand','similarly','second','another method','by contrast','turning to']),('Hangisinin daha etkili olduğunu söyle ve gerekçelendir',['more effective','most effective','in my view','i would argue','on balance','ultimately']),('Akademik bağlaçlar kullan',['however','furthermore','moreover','nevertheless','consequently','in conclusion'])),
  'min':220,'max':260,
  'model':'Many city centres have become places that people pass through rather than places where they want to spend time. Of the possible solutions, banning private cars and creating more green spaces are two of the most frequently discussed.\n\nRemoving cars from the centre has obvious benefits. Air quality improves, streets become quieter, and pedestrians can move around safely. However, opponents argue that shops will lose customers who are used to driving into town. This concern is not unreasonable, but evidence from cities such as Ghent and Pontevedra suggests that footfall often increases once streets are pedestrianised, provided that good public transport is available.\n\nCreating more green spaces addresses a different problem. Parks and tree-lined squares make a city more attractive, encouraging people to linger, meet friends and support local businesses. They also help to reduce temperatures during increasingly frequent heatwaves. The difficulty is that space in city centres is scarce and expensive, so new parks are often small or located on the edges.\n\nOn balance, I would argue that banning private cars is the more effective measure, largely because it makes the second possible. Once roads and car parks are no longer needed, they can be turned into green spaces, cycle lanes and outdoor cafés. In other words, reducing traffic does not simply remove a problem; it creates opportunities. Nevertheless, such a ban must be introduced gradually and accompanied by investment in public transport, or it risks harming the very businesses it is meant to help.'},
 {'choice':[
  {'id':'c1x-w2','part':2,'kind':'proposal','t':'Part 2 · Öneri: Öğrenci kafeteryası',
   'in':'The principal of your college has asked students to submit proposals for improving the college canteen. Write a proposal describing the current problems, suggesting improvements and explaining how these would benefit students. Write 220–260 words.',
   'mail':'PROPOSALS INVITED\nHow can we improve our canteen?\nThe college has a small budget available to improve the canteen. Describe the main problems, suggest how the money should be spent, and explain why your suggestions would benefit students.',
   'pts':WP(('Başlıklar kullan (proposal biçimi)',['introduction','current situation','recommendations','conclusion','purpose']),('Sorunları anlat',['problem','currently','at present','queue','limited','crowded']),('Öneriler sun',['recommend','suggest','propose','should','could']),('Faydaları açıkla',['benefit','would allow','would encourage','as a result','improve']))
   ,'min':220,'max':260,
   'model':'Proposal for improving the college canteen\n\nIntroduction\nThe purpose of this proposal is to outline the main problems with the current canteen and to recommend how the available budget could be used to address them. It is based on a short survey of sixty students.\n\nCurrent situation\nThe most common complaint concerns the queues at lunchtime, which often take twenty minutes. Since the lunch break lasts only forty-five minutes, many students either skip lunch or eat in a hurry. In addition, the menu offers few healthy or vegetarian options, and the seating area is cramped and noisy, which makes it an unappealing place to relax.\n\nRecommendations\nFirstly, I recommend installing a second serving counter offering pre-prepared items such as salads and sandwiches. This would significantly reduce waiting times. Secondly, the menu could be expanded to include at least two vegetarian dishes each day, ideally chosen with input from students. Finally, a small part of the budget could be spent on replacing some of the large tables with smaller ones and adding plants and sound-absorbing panels.\n\nBenefits\nThese changes would allow students to eat properly during the break, which would in turn improve their concentration in afternoon classes. A more varied menu would encourage healthier eating habits, while a more pleasant environment would make the canteen a place where students choose to spend time and socialise.\n\nConclusion\nWith relatively modest investment, the canteen could become a far more efficient and welcoming space. I strongly recommend that these proposals be considered.'},
  {'id':'c1x-w3','part':2,'kind':'review','t':'Part 2 · İnceleme: Bir belgesel',
   'in':'A website is inviting readers to submit reviews of documentaries that have changed the way they think about an issue. Write a review describing the documentary, explaining how it changed your thinking and saying whether you would recommend it. Write 220–260 words.',
   'mail':'REVIEWS WANTED\nA documentary that changed my mind\nTell us about a documentary that made you see an issue differently. What was it about? How did it change your thinking? Would you recommend it to others?',
   'pts':WP(('Belgeseli tanıt',['documentary','follows','explores','about','director']),('Düşüncenin nasıl değiştiğini açıkla',['used to think','changed','realised','made me','before watching']),('Güçlü / zayıf yönleri değerlendir',['however','although','powerful','weakness','strength','criticism']),('Tavsiye et',['recommend','worth','anyone who','must-see']))
   ,'min':220,'max':260,
   'model':'The hidden cost of fast fashion\n\nBefore watching "Threads", I considered myself a fairly responsible consumer. I recycled, I avoided plastic bags, and I rarely gave a second thought to the clothes I bought. This quietly devastating documentary changed that completely.\n\nThe film follows a single cotton T-shirt from a field in Central Asia, through a factory in Bangladesh, to a shop window in London and, eventually, to a mountain of discarded clothing in Ghana. Rather than lecturing the audience, the director lets the people involved tell their own stories: a farmer struggling with polluted water, a seamstress working fourteen-hour days, a trader who sorts through bales of unwanted clothes from Europe.\n\nWhat struck me most was not any single shocking fact, but the cumulative effect. By the end, I could no longer see a cheap T-shirt as a bargain; I saw it as a chain of decisions whose consequences fall on people I will never meet. I have since cut down drastically on the clothes I buy and started repairing rather than replacing.\n\nIf the film has a weakness, it is that it offers few practical solutions, leaving viewers feeling somewhat powerless. A short final section on what individuals and governments can do would have made it even more effective.\n\nNevertheless, I would recommend "Threads" to anyone who buys clothes – which is to say, everyone. It is beautifully filmed, deeply human and, unlike many documentaries on the subject, never preachy. You may not look at your wardrobe in the same way again.'}]}]

SX=[{'id':'c1x-s1','p':1,'t':'Part 1 · Tanışma','ins':'Sınav görevlisi seninle ilgili sorular soruyor. Doğal, gelişmiş ve örnekli cevaplar ver.','q':[
  ['What do you find most rewarding about your studies or work at the moment?',30,['what I find most rewarding','I\'d say','particularly'],"I'd say what I find most rewarding is the feeling of finally understanding something complex. I'm studying economics, and there's a particular satisfaction when a theory suddenly makes sense of something in the news."],
  ['If you could live in any period of history, which would you choose and why?',40,['I\'d be tempted','I\'d have to say','largely because','having said that'],"I'd be tempted to choose the late nineteenth century, largely because so many inventions we take for granted were appearing for the first time. Having said that, I'm not sure I'd cope without modern medicine."],
  ['How do you usually make important decisions?',30,['I tend to','weigh up','gut feeling','in the end'],"I tend to weigh up the pros and cons quite carefully, sometimes even writing them down. But in the end, I often find myself going with my gut feeling anyway."]]},
 {'id':'c1x-s2','p':2,'t':'Part 2 · Fotoğraflar: zor koşullarda çalışmak','ins':'Üç fotoğraftan ikisini seç ve yaklaşık 1 dakika karşılaştır. Soruların ikisini de cevapla.',
  'img':[['🚒🔥🧑‍🚒','Fotoğraf 1: Bir yangına müdahale eden itfaiyeciler.'],['🏔️🧗‍♀️❄️','Fotoğraf 2: Karlı bir dağda kurtarma ekibi.'],['🏥🩺🌙','Fotoğraf 3: Gece vardiyasında yoğun bir acil serviste çalışan doktor.']],
  'q':[['Look at the pictures. They show people working in demanding situations. Compare two of the pictures and say what skills these people need, and how they might be feeling.',60,['whereas','both','they would need','I\'d imagine','under pressure','judging by'],"I'll compare the firefighters and the doctor in the emergency department. Both are clearly working under enormous pressure, where mistakes could cost lives. The firefighters need physical strength and courage, and above all the ability to work as a team, since they rely on each other completely. The doctor, by contrast, needs to make rapid decisions while staying calm and focused, often after hours without sleep. I'd imagine the firefighters are feeling a rush of adrenaline, whereas the doctor might be experiencing a kind of quiet exhaustion, judging by the fact that it's the middle of the night."],
   ['Which of these jobs do you think is the most stressful? Why?',30,['I\'d say','arguably','because'],"I'd say the doctor's, arguably, because the pressure never really stops – a fire eventually ends, but an emergency department is busy every single night."]]},
 {'id':'c1x-s3','p':3,'t':'Part 3 · Birlikte karar verme','ins':'Gerçek sınavda partnerle yapılır. Önce seçenekleri tartış (2 dakika), sonra karar ver (1 dakika).',
  'opts':['📱 social media','📰 traditional newspapers','👨‍👩‍👧 family','🎓 teachers','🎬 celebrities'],
  'q':[['Here are some things that can influence young people\'s opinions. Talk together about how much influence these things have on young people\'s opinions today.',120,['I\'d argue','to a certain extent','that\'s a fair point','it could be argued','whereas','undoubtedly'],"I'd argue social media is undoubtedly the biggest influence today, simply because young people spend so much time on it, and algorithms tend to show them more of what they already believe. Traditional newspapers, by contrast, have much less influence than they once did, although their content is often shared online. Family is still important, especially on values, but perhaps less so on specific issues. Teachers can have a profound influence on some students, particularly in encouraging them to question things. And celebrities – to a certain extent they influence fashion and lifestyle, but I'm not sure young people take their political views very seriously."],
   ['Now you have about a minute to decide which of these has the most positive influence on young people.',60,['if we had to choose','on balance','I\'d go for','even though'],"If we had to choose, I'd go for teachers. Even though their influence is less constant than social media's, it tends to be more thoughtful, and good teachers encourage young people to form their own opinions rather than simply adopting someone else's."]]},
 {'id':'c1x-s4','p':4,'t':'Part 4 · Tartışma','ins':'3. bölümün konusuyla ilgili genel sorular. Görüşünü gerekçelerle ve farklı bakış açılarını da değerlendirerek açıkla.','q':[
  ['Do you think young people today are better or worse at thinking critically than previous generations?',45,['it\'s debatable','on the one hand','by the same token','I suspect'],"It's debatable. On the one hand, they have access to far more information and are used to questioning sources. By the same token, though, the sheer quantity of information can be overwhelming, and I suspect many people rely on headlines rather than reading in depth."],
  ['Should schools teach students how to identify false information online?',45,['absolutely','it\'s essential','not least because','in my view'],"Absolutely. In my view, it's as essential today as learning to read, not least because false information can have real consequences, from health decisions to elections."],
  ['To what extent should people with influence take responsibility for what they say?',45,['to a large extent','that said','there\'s a balance','freedom of expression'],"To a large extent, I think they should, because their words reach millions of people. That said, there's a balance to be struck with freedom of expression – we can't expect everyone to be an expert on every topic."]]}]

