PW=[
 {'id':'c1w1','part':1,'kind':'essay','t':'Deneme: Sanata kamu desteği','in':'Your class has watched a discussion about how governments should support the arts. Write an essay discussing TWO of the methods in your notes, explaining which you think is more important and giving reasons. Write 220–260 words.','mail':'Ways governments can support the arts:\n• funding museums and galleries\n• providing arts education in schools\n• giving grants to individual artists\n\nOpinions expressed:\n"Museums should be free so that everyone can enjoy them."\n"Children who never learn an instrument miss out on so much."\n"Artists shouldn\'t depend on government money."',
  'pts':WP(('İlk yöntemi tartış',['museum','gallery','education','school','grant','artist']),('İkinci yöntemi tartış',['whereas','on the other hand','by contrast','turning to','second']),('Hangisinin daha önemli olduğunu gerekçelendir',['more important','in my view','i would argue','on balance','ultimately']),('Akademik bağlaçlar',['however','furthermore','moreover','nevertheless','consequently'])),'min':220,'max':260,
  'model':'Public money for the arts is often among the first things to be cut when budgets are tight. Yet there are strong arguments for continued support, particularly through funding museums and providing arts education in schools.\n\nFunding museums and galleries ensures that a country\'s cultural heritage is preserved and accessible. When entry is free, as it is in many British museums, visitor numbers rise dramatically and include people who would never otherwise attend. However, free museums mainly benefit those who live nearby and already have an interest in culture; they do little to create new audiences.\n\nArts education in schools, by contrast, reaches every child, regardless of background. Learning an instrument, acting in a play or studying painting develops creativity, confidence and the ability to collaborate – skills that are valuable far beyond the arts. Moreover, children who discover a passion for the arts early are likely to become the museum visitors, concert-goers and even artists of the future.\n\nOn balance, I would argue that arts education is the more important of the two. While museums preserve the culture of the past, education creates the culture of the future. Without it, the arts risk becoming the preserve of a privileged minority who can afford private lessons. That is not to say museums should be neglected, but if governments must prioritise, investing in children\'s creative development is likely to have the greater long-term impact on society as a whole.'},
 {'id':'c1w2','part':1,'kind':'essay','t':'Deneme: Yaşlanan nüfus','in':'Your class has attended a lecture on the challenges of an ageing population. Write an essay discussing TWO of the issues in your notes, explaining which you think governments should address first, giving reasons. Write 220–260 words.','mail':'Challenges of an ageing population:\n• healthcare costs\n• pensions\n• loneliness among older people\n\nOpinions expressed:\n"We can\'t afford to keep paying for longer retirements."\n"Hospitals are already under pressure."\n"Isolation can be as harmful as smoking."',
  'pts':WP(('İlk konuyu tartış',['healthcare','hospital','pension','retire','lonely','loneliness','isolation']),('İkinci konuyu tartış',['whereas','on the other hand','by contrast','turning to']),('Öncelikli olanı gerekçelendir',['first','priority','more urgent','in my view','i would argue']),('Akademik bağlaçlar',['however','furthermore','moreover','nevertheless','consequently'])),'min':220,'max':260,
  'model':'As life expectancy rises and birth rates fall, many countries face the challenge of supporting a growing number of older citizens. Two of the most pressing issues are the rising cost of healthcare and the problem of loneliness.\n\nHealthcare costs are perhaps the most visible concern. Older people are more likely to suffer from long-term conditions, and hospitals in many countries are already struggling to cope. Without reform, governments may have to choose between raising taxes and reducing the quality of care, neither of which is politically attractive.\n\nLoneliness, by contrast, receives far less attention, yet its effects are serious. Research suggests that chronic isolation increases the risk of heart disease and dementia, and some studies have compared its impact to that of smoking. As families become more scattered, many older people spend days without speaking to anyone.\n\nI would argue that governments should address loneliness first, not because it is more important than healthcare, but because the two are closely connected. Older people who remain socially active tend to stay healthier for longer and need less medical treatment. Consequently, relatively inexpensive measures such as community centres, volunteer visiting schemes and intergenerational projects could reduce pressure on hospitals. Furthermore, these measures can be introduced quickly, whereas reforming healthcare systems takes years. Tackling isolation is therefore not only the humane choice but also a practical investment in the sustainability of public services.'},
 {'id':'c1w3','part':2,'kind':'report','t':'Rapor: Öğrenci kulüpleri','in':'The director of your college wants to know how the student clubs could be improved. Write a report evaluating the current clubs, identifying any problems and making recommendations. Write 220–260 words.','mail':'REPORT REQUEST\nStudent clubs\nPlease evaluate the current range of student clubs, explain any problems that limit participation, and recommend changes that would encourage more students to take part.',
  'pts':WP(('Başlıklar kullan (report biçimi)',['introduction','findings','recommendations','conclusion','aim']),('Mevcut durumu değerlendir',['currently','at present','range','popular','participation']),('Sorunları belirt',['problem','however','limited','lack','clash']),('Öneriler sun',['recommend','suggest','should','it would be advisable'])),'min':220,'max':260,
  'model':'Report on student clubs\n\nIntroduction\nThe aim of this report is to evaluate the college\'s current student clubs and to suggest ways of increasing participation. It is based on a questionnaire completed by 85 students and interviews with four club leaders.\n\nCurrent situation\nThe college offers twelve clubs, ranging from debating to photography. The sports clubs and the drama society are well attended and highly rated. However, overall participation is low: only around a third of students belong to a club, and several clubs have fewer than five regular members.\n\nMain problems\nThe most frequently mentioned problem was timing. Most clubs meet immediately after classes, which clashes with part-time jobs and long journeys home. A second issue is awareness; many students, particularly first-years, were unaware that some clubs existed. Finally, a number of respondents felt that the range of clubs did not reflect current interests, with no options related to technology, the environment or mental well-being.\n\nRecommendations\nFirstly, it would be advisable to hold some club meetings at lunchtime, when most students are on site. Secondly, a club fair at the start of each term, together with a regularly updated online page, would raise awareness. Thirdly, students should be invited to propose new clubs, with a small budget available for those that attract sufficient interest.\n\nConclusion\nIf these changes are implemented, I am confident that participation would increase significantly, enriching students\' college experience.'},
 {'id':'c1w4','part':2,'kind':'proposal','t':'Öneri: Kasaba festivali','in':'Your town council is planning to hold an annual festival and has asked residents for proposals. Write a proposal suggesting what the festival should include, explaining how it would benefit the town and addressing any potential problems. Write 220–260 words.','mail':'PROPOSALS WANTED\nA new town festival\nWhat kind of festival would bring the community together and attract visitors? Explain your ideas, how they would benefit the town, and how any problems could be dealt with.',
  'pts':WP(('Başlıklar kullan (proposal biçimi)',['introduction','proposal','benefits','potential problems','conclusion']),('Festival içeriği öner',['include','feature','stalls','music','food','workshop']),('Faydaları açıkla',['benefit','local businesses','community','attract','boost']),('Olası sorunlara çözüm',['problem','concern','could be addressed','to avoid','solution'])),'min':220,'max':260,
  'model':'Proposal for an annual town festival\n\nIntroduction\nThis proposal outlines a festival celebrating the town\'s food and craft traditions, which I believe would bring residents together and attract visitors from the surrounding area.\n\nWhat the festival should include\nI propose a two-day weekend event held in the main square and along the riverside. The centrepiece would be a market featuring local food producers, bakers and craftspeople. Alongside this, there could be cooking demonstrations by local restaurants, workshops where children learn traditional crafts such as pottery and weaving, and live music in the evenings performed by local bands and school groups.\n\nBenefits for the town\nThe festival would give local businesses valuable publicity and additional income. It would also strengthen the community by involving schools, clubs and residents of all ages. In the longer term, a successful festival could put the town on the map as a destination, encouraging tourism throughout the year.\n\nPotential problems\nResidents in the centre may be concerned about noise and parking. These concerns could be addressed by ending live music at 10 p.m. and providing a free shuttle bus from car parks on the edge of town. Another risk is bad weather; a covered area near the town hall should therefore be reserved for key events.\n\nConclusion\nWith careful planning, a food and craft festival would be an affordable and inclusive way to celebrate the town\'s identity. I strongly recommend that the council consider this proposal.'},
 {'id':'c1w5','part':2,'kind':'letter','t':'Mektup: Gazeteye görüş','in':'You have read an article in a local newspaper arguing that the town library should be closed to save money. Write a letter to the editor responding to the article and explaining why the library should remain open. Write 220–260 words.','mail':'From the article:\n"In an age when information is available online, libraries are an expensive luxury. The council should close the town library and use the money for more urgent needs."',
  'pts':WP(('Uygun hitap ve kapanış',['dear','editor','yours faithfully','yours sincerely']),('Makaleye yanıt ver',['article','claim','argue','suggests','i was']),('Kütüphanenin faydalarını açıkla',['library','free','community','access','children','elderly']),('Güçlü bir sonuç',['urge','strongly','therefore','in conclusion','i hope'])),'min':220,'max':260,
  'model':'Dear Editor,\n\nI am writing in response to last week\'s article suggesting that the town library should be closed to save money. While I understand the pressure on the council\'s budget, I believe the article seriously underestimates the role the library plays in our community.\n\nThe writer claims that libraries are unnecessary because information is available online. This overlooks the fact that many residents do not have reliable internet access at home. For them, the library\'s free computers are essential for applying for jobs, accessing government services and helping children with homework.\n\nFurthermore, the library is far more than a collection of books. It hosts reading groups for children, language classes for newcomers and a weekly meeting for elderly residents, many of whom live alone. For these people, the library is one of the few warm, safe and free public spaces left in the town.\n\nThe article also describes the library as an "expensive luxury". Yet its annual budget is a tiny fraction of the council\'s total spending, and the cost of closing it – in terms of increased isolation, reduced literacy and fewer opportunities – would be far greater in the long run.\n\nRather than closing the library, the council could explore ways of making it more financially sustainable, such as renting rooms to local groups or seeking support from businesses.\n\nI therefore urge the council to reject this proposal and to recognise the library as the valuable public service it is.\n\nYours faithfully,\nZeynep Arslan'}]

